J Ophthalmol</source><year>1967</year><volume>63</volume><fpage>1796</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5298081</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R6"><label>6</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>AG</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Hsu</surname><given-names>WM</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>AF</given-names></name><name><surname>Yen</surname><given-names>MY</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Optic neuritis in herpes zoster ophthalmicus</article-title><source>Jpn J Ophthalmol</source><year>2000</year><volume>44</volume><fpage>550</fpage><lpage>4</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11033135</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R7"><label>7</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schmidt</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Herpes zoster ophthalmicus with retrobalbar neuritis: a case report</article-title><source>Acta Ophthalmol</source><year>1983</year><volume>61</volume><fpage>501</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6605018</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R8"><label>8</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kattah</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Kennerdelt</surname><given-names>JS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Orbital apex syndrome secondary to herpes zoster ophthalmicus</article-title><source>Am J Ophthalmol</source><year>1978</year><volume>85</volume><fpage>378</fpage><lpage>82</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">306754</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R9"><label>9</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Scharf</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Kraus</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Zonis</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Optic neuropathy and central retinal artery occulusion in a case of herpes zoster ophthalmicus</article-title><source>Ann Ophthalmol</source><year>1987</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>77</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3566029</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R10"><label>10</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Quisling</surname><given-names>SV</given-names></name><name><surname>Shah</surname><given-names>VA</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>HK</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Magnetic resonance imaging of third cranial nerve palsy and trigeminal sensory loss caused by herpes zoster</article-title><source>J Neuroophthalmol</source><year>2006</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>47</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16518167</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R11"><label>11</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Naumann</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Gass</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Font</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Histopathology of herpes zoster ophthalmicus</article-title><source>Am J Ophthalmol</source><year>1968</year><volume>65</volume><fpage>533</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5300433</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R12"><label>12</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Harrison</surname><given-names>EQ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Complications of herpes zoater ophthalmicus</article-title><source> Am J Ophthalmol</source><year>1965</year><volume>60</volume><fpage>1111</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5295201</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. (1)</label><caption><p>Images obtained by magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) in an eye with orbital apex syndrome. <bold>(A)</bold> Horizontal MRI, T<sub>1</sub>-weighted fat suppressed image. <bold>(B)</bold> Horizontal MRI, T<sub>1</sub>-weighted with Gadolinium enhanced image. <bold>(C)</bold> Horizontal MRI, T<sub>2</sub>-weighted and horizontal image. <bold>(D)</bold> Coronal MRI, T<sub>1</sub>-weighted fat suppressed image. <bold>(E)</bold>. Coronal MRI, T<sub>1</sub>-weighted with Gadolinium enhanced image. <bold>(F)</bold> Coronal MRI, T2-weighted image. The right orbital cavity, external ocular muscles, and optic nerve are highly enhanced in the T<sub>2</sub>-weighted images and in the T<sub>1</sub>-weighted with Gadolinium enhanced images.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOOPHTJ-2-109_F1"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. (2)</label><caption><p>Pattern VECPs elicited by stimulating the right affected eye (upper) and left eye (lower). VECPs are non-recordable when the right affected eye was stimulated while the P100 component is clearly recorded with left eye.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOOPHTJ-2-109_F2"/></fig></floats-group></article>